 guess: how[87Gmany scop[97Gs this has to serve.[118GIf [38;2;177;185;249mharness[39m [130Gs th[135G only real group, Layer[159G2 and the T-Box[175Gscope filter are sp[195Gculativ[203G and I'd cut them today. If [38;2;177;185;249mcareer-ops[39m and[246G[K[2C[1B[38;2;177;185;249mTrading-Bot[39m are[19Gjoining,[28Gthe filt[37Gr is a prerequisite, not a[64Gnice-to-have.[2B[38;2;153;153;153m● [39mUser answered Claude's[26Gquestions:[K[1B[38;2;153;153;153m  ⎿  · Which project groups is this ontology actually going to serve? Right now the DB has exactly one real scope — `harness` (arteries, capillaries, marrow) — plus 270 junk /tmp rows. `heart` and `plexus` exist on disk but aren't tracked. → Harness + [5C[1Bmany, growing over time[1B[39m[K[1B[38;2;255;255;255m●[39m [1mSettled:[22m Layer 2 and the T-Box scope filter are prerequisites, not extension points — I'll treat [38;2;177;185;249montology_bindings[39m + a scope-filtered [38;2;177;185;249m_lookup()[39m as blocking work before any domain vocabulary gets loaded. It also means the vocabulary authorship loop and[2C[1Ba[5Ggrowth budget are part of the design, not an afterthought.[K[2B [3G[1mOpened:[22m at ~20[18Gtracked repos, [34Gcope [3mgranularity[23m stops being cosm[68Gti[71G. Entities are unique per [38;2;177;185;249m(scope_id, kind, lower(name))[39m, so the number of scopes i[154G exactly the number of times a[185Gshared noun[197Glike[202G[38;2;177;185;249mpgvector[39m or [38;2;177;185;249mPostg[220Ges[39m gets duplicat[239Gas an unrelated[2C[1Bnode — and duplicated nodes can't produce the shared-entity t[66Gversal that [38;2;177;185;249mexpand()[39m de[91Gnds [97G (your[104Glive[109Ggraph:[116G68[119G[38;2;177;185;249mmentions[128G[39medges[134Gdoing[140Gthe[144Gheavy[150Glifting[158Gagainst[166G33[169Gdirect[176Gclaim-to-claim[191Gedges).[2B[38;2;153;153;153m● [39mUser answered Claude's questions:[K[1B[38;2;153;153;153m  ⎿  · At ~20 tracked repos, how should scopes be cut? This decides the entity namespace — and it's the expensive one to change later, since regrouping means re-keying entity nodes and re-deriving their edges. → Scopes should follow whatever group I [39m[K[1B     [38;2;153;153;153mdefine. Some repos are their own scopes (only one repo in group) while others like harness contain multiple repos (capillaries, arteries, heart, plexus, marrow noting I haven't done more work on heart, plexus and marrow yet) [2B[38;2;255;255;255m●[3G[39m[1mSettled:[22m scope membership stays hand-defined — which is already exactly what [38;2;177;185;249mart scope add[39m does, so no mechanism changes. Cardinality is arbitrary: mostly singletons, [38;2;177;185;249mharness[39m as the one cluster (and it[205Gshould[212Gget[216G[38;2;177;185;249mheart[222G[39mand[226G[38;2;177;185;249mplexus[233G[39madded[239Gnow,[244Geven[2C[1Bunworked,[13Gso[16Gtheir[22Gfirst[28Gcommit[35Glands[41Gin[44Gthe[48Gright[54Gnamespace[64Grather[71Gthan[76Gminting[84Ga[86Gscope-of-one).[2C[1B[K[2C[1B[1mConsequence to park:[22m with mostly-singleton scopes, a cross-cutting noun like [38;2;177;185;249mpgvector[39m or [38;2;177;185;249mPostgres[39m becomes ~15 unrelated nodes. Whether that's a problem depends entirely on the next answer, so I'm not designing around it yet.[K[2C[1B[K[1B[38;2;153;153;153m● [39mUser answered Claude's questions:[K[1B[38;2;153;153;153m  ⎿  · Six months from now, what is the ONE question you most want to type and get a real answer to? Ontology is driven by query — this decides which relations get built and which linkage work is worth doing. → 1 because that helps with building out [5C[1Bprojects [17G noncontradictory[35Gways ([42Gx. building feature A at first w[75Gth details 1, 2,[92G3[94Gbut w[100Gnting to build featur[122G B la[128Ger with details 4, 5[150Gonflicting with 1,[169G2 meani[