oaded. It also means the vocabulary authorship loop and[2C[1Ba[5Ggrowth budget are part of the design, not an afterthought.[K[2B [3G[1mOpened:[22m at ~20[18Gtracked repos, [34Gcope [3mgranularity[23m stops being cosm[68Gti[71G. Entities are unique per [38;2;177;185;249m(scope_id, kind, lower(name))[39m, so the number of scopes i[154G exactly the number of times a[185Gshared noun[197Glike[202G[38;2;177;185;249mpgvector[39m or [38;2;177;185;249mPostg[220Ges[39m gets duplicat[239Gas an unrelated[2C[1Bnode — and duplicated nodes can't produce the shared-entity t[66Gversal that [38;2;177;185;249mexpand()[39m de[91Gnds [97G (your[104Glive[109Ggraph:[116G68[119G[38;2;177;185;249mmentions[128G[39medges[134Gdoing[140Gthe[144Gheavy[150Glifting[158Gagainst[166G33[169Gdirect[176Gclaim-to-claim[191Gedges).[2B[38;2;153;153;153m● [39mUser answered Claude's questions:[K[1B[38;2;153;153;153m  ⎿  · At ~20 tracked repos, how should scopes be cut? This decides the entity namespace — and it's the expensive one to change later, since regrouping means re-keying entity nodes and re-deriving their edges. → Scopes should follow whatever group I [1B[39m     [38;2;153;153;153mdefine. Some repos are their own scopes (only one repo in group) while others like harness contain multiple repos (capillaries, arteries, heart, plexus, marrow noting I haven't done more work on heart, plexus and marrow yet) [1B[39m[K[1B[38;2;255;255;255m●[3G[39m[1mSettled:[22m scope membership stays hand-defined — which is already exactly what [38;2;177;185;249mart scope add[39m does, so no mechanism changes. Cardinality is arbitrary: mostly singletons, [38;2;177;185;249mharness[39m as the one cluster (and it should get [38;2;177;185;249mheart[39m and [38;2;177;185;249mplexus[39m added now, even[2C[1Bunworked,[13Gso[16Gtheir[22Gfirst[28Gcommit[35Glands[41Gin[44Gthe[48Gright[54Gnamespace[64Grather[71Gthan[76Gminting[84Ga[86Gscope-of-one).[1B[K[2C[1B[1mConsequence to park:[24G[22mwith[29Gmostly-singleton[46Gscopes,[54Ga[56Gcross-cutting[70Gnoun[75Glike[80G[38;2;177;185;249mpgvector[89G[39mor[92G[38;2;177;185;249mPostgres[101G[39mbecomes[109G~15[113Gunrelated[123Gnodes.[130GWhether[138Gthat's[145Ga[147Gproblem[155Gdepends[163Gentirely[172Gon[175Gthe[179Gnext[184Ganswer,[192Gso[195GI'm[199Gnot[203Gdesigning[213Garound[220Git[223Gyet.[2C[1B[K[1B[38;2;153;153;153m● [39mUser answered Claude's questions:[K[1B[38;2;153;153;153m  ⎿  · Six months from now, what is the ONE question you most want to type and get a real answer to? Ontology is driven by query — this decides which relations get built and which linkage work is worth doing. → 1 because that helps with building out [5C[1Bprojects in noncontradictory ways (ex. building feature A at first with details 1, 2, 3 but wanting to build feature B later with details 4, 5 conflicting with 1, 2 meaning I need help resolving these issues) but I'd like all 4 eventually[2C[1B[39m[K[1B[38;2;255;255;255m●[3G[39m[1mSettled, and it sharpens the whole design.[46G[22mYou[50Gdon't[56Gwant[61Gdecision[70G[3mrecall[77G[23m—[79Gyou[83Gwant[88G[1mconflict detection at design time[22m:[123Gwhen[128Gyou[132Gspec[137Gfeature[145GB,[148Gthe[152Ggraph[158Gmust[163Gsurface[171Gthe[175Gprior[181Gdecisions[191Gand[195Gconstraints[207Gthat[212Gdetails[220G4[222Gand[226G5[228Gviolate.[1B[K[1B [3GThat changes w[18Gat the ontology[34Ghas[38Gto[41Gmake[46Gwork.[52GCosine[59Gsimilarity[70Gwon't[76Gfind[81Gthese[87G—[89G"feature[98GA[100Gkeys[105Gmemories[114Gby[117GUUID"[123Gand[127G"feature[136GB[138Gkeys[143Gby[146Grepo[151Gpath"[157Gare[161Gsemantically[174Gdistant[182Gbut[186Gstructurally[199Gin[202Gconflict.[212GWhat[217Gfinds[223Gthem[228Gis[231Gthe[235G[1mshared-entity arm of[2C[1B[38;2;177;185;249mexpand()[12G[22m[3